% c262 (A&S Unit 27, PDF 463-464 Ex. 27-1, 27-2: the 9th and 7th of V9 resolve in parallel into 5 and 3 of I; PDF 465-466 and 479 point 4: unresolved 7ths are very rare): 72 beats 3-4 the violas pass D4-C4-B flat3 (major 9th of B flat7) while the cellos take the 7th A flat3 to G3 at 73 (it rose to B flat3), so c232's fifth chain C7-Fm7-B flat9-E flat resolves its last 7th.
\version "2.24.0"
% c462 (A&S Unit 10, PDF 153-155, 160 points 2, 3, 7; Ex. 10-3a, 10-4a): 82 beats 3-4 cadential 6/4 -> V before the E flat cadence at 83. iv6 (C flat bass) now lasts beats 1-2; the bass (Cb, Vc, Bn 2, Vla) moves to B flat on beat 3; Hn 1 keeps E flat4 as the 4th (common tone from iv6) falling to D4 on beat 4; Bn 1 A flat3-G3-F3 (6th to 5th); Hn 2 B flat2 from beat 3. The tune's G5 on 82.3 is now the 6th of the chord instead of clashing with A flat over C flat.
% c126 (Adler PDF 188: give the flutist time to breathe in sustained passages): 78 breath for Fl 1 and Ob 1 at the slur end (77-86 was 14.7 s without a rest, ending in the 82 run straight into 83).
% c232 (harmony; A&S Unit 25, PDF 428 Ex. 25-12, PDF 419, 424, 439; Unit 24, PDF 399-400, Unit 25, PDF 437): 80 beats 1-2 V6/5 of V (A natural-C-E flat-F) between IV (79) and V (80.3): Cb and Bn 2 A natural (sharp 4 rising to 5), Bn 1 F3, Hn 2 F3 held, harp RH F4-C5-E flat5-F5 and LH rest (no pedal change); the violas' and cellos' E flat4 is the seventh and falls to D4. 70 beats 3-4 V4/3 of ii (G-C-E natural-B flat) for I6, so 70-73 runs C7-Fm7-B flat7-E flat by descending fifths and the tune's B flat resolves as a seventh to A flat at 71: the violas' and cellos' counter-melody takes E natural4 (was G4) and falls chromatically to E flat4, the seventh of ii7 (one voice, no cross relation; A&S PDF 424 Ex. 25-3a, PDF 433), which falls to D4 at 72; Bn 1 holds C3 (was C3-E flat3), harp RH C4-G4-B flat4-C5 (no E, no pedal change). The tune is unchanged.
% SECOND THEME IN MAJOR, bars 67-82, E flat major: theme F2 in the violins in octaves (Vln I at B flat 6 at most, 78, c011; movement III's
% C7 is kept for the C-major arrival at 219), the cellos' counter-melody (the bass counterline movement I lacked), harp broken chords,
% violas off the beat. Flute 1 and oboe 1 join the tune from 77 for its climax. 81-82 animando into the closing group.

% c032 (harmony): 79 Cl 2 and Hn 2 C4 (were A flat3: B flat-A flat under the tune's F-E flat, parallel 5ths).
% c095: the violins' crescendo to f at 78 starts on the downbeat of 77 (was on the last quarter of 76, which ends a system: the hairpin printed as a 2 mm stub after the last note, then restarted on the next system).
% c141 (melody; Sebesky ch. 6, PDF 210, Ex. 277 vs 278: a lead line in even values has less character; PDF 207-208: a change of note values carries the line into the next phrase): 78 the peak B flat takes F1's dotted rhythm (dotted quarter, eighth: bes4. aes8 g4 f4) in Vln I-II, Fl 1, Ob 1, so the climax bar no longer has the same four even quarters as 68, 72, 76 and 80; the down-bow peak (c018) is kept. The trumpet solo (62) and its recapitulation (182) stay plain (protected).
% c141 (Sebesky PDF 209, Ex. 275: passing or neighbour tones in the lower of two parallel lines): 81 Vla/Vc A flat-B flat-A flat-F in quarters (was A flat-F in halves, parallel sixths with Vln II F4-D4); beat 4 F rises to G at 82, so it does not double Hn 2's A flat3-G3.
% c165 (MOLA PDF 8: proofread the parts as the player reads them; as c065 for the violas at 171): Vln I has not played pizz. anywhere before 67, so "arco" told
% them to change something they were not doing; "cantabile" stays.
% c241 (melody; A&S Unit 21, PDF 353-354 sec. 15 and Ex. 21-15e/f: the 7/6 over V7, 'rather frequently, especially in nineteenth-century music, a delay of 2 over V7';
%   PDF 348 secs. 3-4: the suspension is stressed, its resolution falls by step and is softer; PDF 352 sec. 12: the resolution tone F4/F3 in harp and Hn 2 is an octave or more lower in another timbre):
%   80-81 Vln I, Vln II and Fl 1 tie the tune's G across the barline over the V7 of 81 (7th A flat3 in cellos, violas and Bn 1), resolving to F on 81 beat 2 (g4 f4 d2, was f2 d2).
%   F2's only full cadence in the major now differs from the trumpet's minor statement (64-65, unchanged).
% c248 (expression; A&S Unit 3, PDF 54-55, Ex. 3-16 and 3-18: the suspension is the accented dissonance on the strong beat, its resolution is the lighter note;
%   PDF 53: 81 is the strong seventh bar of 75-82): Vln I, Vln II and Fl 1 hold their level through the suspended G (80.4 tied into 81.1) and begin the diminuendo
%   on the resolution F (81.2); it began on the preparation at 80.4, so the dissonance was softer than the note that prepared it (c241 tied the G; the hairpin stayed). mf/mp at 82 unchanged.
% c261 (A&S Unit 3, PDF 56 Ex. 3-19: anticipation tied over the strong beat; PDF 51 syncopation; Beethoven 9/iv 103-104): 73.4 the tune's F
% enters a beat early, tied into 74's half cadence (was g2 es2 | f1); 81's suspension (c241) answers it. Same in the recapitulation at 193-194.
% c271 (melody; A&S Unit 20, PDF 338-339 sec. 13, Ex. 20-21b, Mahler: in nineteenth-century music the turn becomes a five-note figure, main tone - upper neighbour - main tone - lower neighbour - main tone;
%   PDF 333 sec. 6: the neighbour figure is often treated motivically; corpus Dvorak 9/iv 39 (Cl 1, A4.-G16-A4 on the varied repeat of 35-36)): 75 the second half of the E flat-major F2 opens with
%   B flat5 - C6 - B flat5 - A flat5 - B flat5 (16ths on beat 3, one slur) before G5, in Vln I and Vln II (octave lower), so 75-76 no longer repeat 67-68 note for note. Diatonic A flat, not A natural:
%   the violas and cellos hold A flat3 on that beat. Trumpet solo (51-66), 187-198 and every other bar of F2 unchanged.
% c273 (form; A&S Unit 14, PDF 215 and Ex. 14-2, 14-3d: the typical approach to a semicadence is the bass 4-sharp 4-5 under IV and V6/5 of V; 'a particularly effective semicadence', where
%   a diatonic approach leaves V 'wanting to continue'; PDF 214 NOTE: sharp 4 is not doubled; Dvorak 9/iv 60-67, vii-7 of V into the second group): 73 beats 3-4 the half cadence of F2's
%   E flat-major antecedent is approached by IV (A flat) and V6/5 of V (A natural-C-E flat-F; the tune's anticipated F is its root) instead of vi: Cb and Bn 2 A flat-A natural rising to B flat at 74,
%   Bn 1 C3-E flat3 (the seventh, falling to D3 at 74), violas and cellos hold C4 and take B flat3 on 74.1 so the seventh A flat3 follows on beat 2 (no direct A natural-A flat cross relation),
%   harp RH E flat4-A flat4-C5-F5 and LH A flat1 then rest (no A natural pedal). Tune, rhythm, dynamics and the recapitulation (193-194, protected) unchanged.
% c312 (A&S Unit 22, PDF 385 sec. 14, Ex. 22-14 (Schubert D. 845): in major VII-half-dim7 'typically occurs as a neighboring chord to I ... frequently with a soprano progression (5) 6 5';
%   PDF 381 sec. 7: harmonizing 6 in 5-6-5 is 'a particularly characteristic and important function'; PDF 383 sec. 10, Ex. 22-10: the leading-tone seventh over a tonic pedal):
%   75 beat 3, under the violins' turn C6-B flat-A flat-B flat (6 over the tonic), the pad becomes D-F-A flat-C over the E flat pedal and returns to I on beat 4:
%   Hn 1 E flat4-D4-E flat4, Hn 2 G3-F3-G3, Bn 1 B flat3-F3-B flat3, harp F4-D4 | G4-B flat4 (was E flat4-G4 | B flat4-E flat5). The counter (violas, cellos A flat3) already had the A flat.
%   The 7th C is in the soprano only, and I on beat 4 doubles its 3rd G (Hn 2, violins), as PDF 386, Ex. 22-15b; no part moves to beat 4 in octaves with another (allpairs_par.py).
%   Before, the violas' and cellos' A flat3 sounded against Hn 2's G3 and Bn 1's B flat3 inside a held tonic (E flat-G-A flat-B flat-C). The tune, bass and dynamics are unchanged.
% c361 (melody; A&S Unit 20, PDF 337 sec. 9 end and Ex. 20-17: 'An important idiom consists of an incomplete neighbor (escape tone) followed by an accented passing tone, the two tones forming the decoration of a melodic progression of a second, usually a descending one'; PDF 333 sec. 6: the neighbour figure 'is often treated motivically'; PDF 338 sec. 13, Ex. 20-21b, the five-note turn of 75):
%   71.4-72.1 Vln I C6-D6 | C6-B flat5 (Vln II an octave lower): the step C-B flat from ii7 (71) to V7 (72) is decorated by the escape tone D (a dissonant sixth over ii7, a seventh above the violas' E flat4) and the accented passing tone C on 72.1 (the ninth over V, resolving to B flat on the off-beat). The figure C-D-C-B flat is the turn of 75 (B flat-C-B flat-A flat) a step higher in eighths, so F2's second phrase now prepares the turn. Same at 191.4-192.1 (recS.ily). Trumpet solo 51-66, 171-186 plain; slurs per bar as before.
sTwoVlnOne = { bes''2.(\mf^\markup \italic "ord., cantabile" g''4) | f''4(\< es''4 g''4 bes''4) | f'''2(\> es'''4 d'''4) | c'''2(\! bes''2) | aes''2.( c'''8 d'''8) | c'''8( bes''8 aes''4 g''4 f''4) | g''2( es''4 f''4~ | f''1\>) | bes''2(\mf c'''16 bes'' aes'' bes'' g''4) | f''4( es''4 g''4 bes''4) | es'''2.\< f'''4\upbow | bes'''4.(\f\downbow aes'''8 g'''4 f'''4) | es'''2.( d'''4) | c'''4( bes''4 aes''4 g''4~) | g''4( f''4\> d''2) | es''2\mf\< g''8( aes''8 bes''8 d'''8) | }
% c165 (MOLA PDF 8, Example 7, PDF 12: anchored marks collide in extracted parts): in the Vln II part "sul G" (81.3) and "ord." (82.1) printed as one phrase,
% "sul G ord."; "sul G" is now a dashed text spanner over the D4 that ends at 82 (as "sul A" at 235), so no "ord." is needed.
sTwoVlnTwo = { bes'2.(\mf^\markup \italic "arco, unis., cantabile" g'4) | f'4(\< es'4 g'4 bes'4) | f''2(\> es''4 d''4) | c''2(\! bes'2) | aes'2.( c''8 d''8) | c''8( bes'8 aes'4 g'4 f'4) | g'2( es'4 f'4~ | f'1\>) | bes'2(\mf c''16 bes' aes' bes' g'4) | f'4( es'4 g'4 bes'4) | es''2.\< f''4\upbow | bes''4.(\f\downbow aes''8 g''4 f''4) | es''2.( d''4) | c''4( bes'4 aes'4 g'4~) | g'4( f'4\> \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "sul G" d'2\startTextSpan) | es'2\stopTextSpan\mf\< g'8( aes'8 bes'8 d''8) | }
% c145 (Adler ch. 18, PDF 295, 301 Ex. XVIII-10a): 77 "1." for Fl 1 and Ob 1 (score only): both enter alone on a shared staff after 26 and 30 bars of rest, the last label being at 43/47.
% c145 (Adler ch. 18, PDF 299 point 6: cues after a long rest, instrument named, small notes, transposed for the part): part-only cues, Ob 1 75-76 (Vln I) before its 77 entry after 26 bars' rest; Hn 1-2 73-74 (Vc.) before their 75 entry after 24 bars' rest across two tempo and key changes.
% c145 (Adler ch. 18, PDF 291 point 3, every detail for every instrument): Hn 1-2 breath at the end of 82; they play 75-86 (about 18 s) without a rest since c144's pedal.
% c167 (texture; Sebesky ch. 3, PDF 164: one woodwind weighs about as much as sixteen violins; a flute on the lead violin line 'thickens the string tone considerably, sacrificing purity', and Sebesky marks the wind one level softer than the strings): 78 Fl 1, Ob 1 and Cl 1-2 reach mf (were f, level with the violins' f at F2's peak, Fl 1 on B flat6 in unison with Vln I); the 82 run in Fl 1 and Ob 1 mp < under the violins' mf <. The violins' dynamics are unchanged.
sTwoFluteOne = { R1*10 | es'''2.(\p\<-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." f'''4 | bes'''4.\mf aes'''8 g'''4 f'''4) \breathe | es'''2.( d'''4 | c'''4 bes''4 aes''4 g''4~ | g''4 f''4\> d''2 | es''2)\mp\< g''8( aes''8 bes''8 d'''8) | }
% c144 (Adler ch. 8, PDF 270, Ex. VIII-41, Copland: the diminuendo is scored by cutting the first oboe, which 'honks' in its extreme low register; ch. 7, PDF 198: the oboe is thick in its lowest fifth and cannot play softly there): 81-82.2 Ob 1 rests (it doubled Vln II's sul G F4-D4-E flat4 under the diminuendo); it re-enters for the run G4-A flat4-B flat4-D5 into 83. Fl 1 keeps the Vln I doubling.
sTwoOboeOne = { R1*8 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vlnOne" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | es''2.(\p\<-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." f''4 | bes''4.\mf aes''8 g''4 f''4) \breathe | es''2.( d''4 | c''4 bes'4 aes'4 es''4~ | es''4 d''2\>) r4\! | r2 g'8(\mp\< aes'8 bes'8 d''8) | }
% c027 (texture; Adler II PDF 259-260): 69-70 the violas' and cellos' counter-melody no longer moves in octaves with the tune (was c'-es'-d' | c'-bes against f-es-d | c-bes); it now moves against the tune's rhythm, A flat-B flat-C | C-D-E flat.
% c051 (melody; Sebesky ch. 6, PDF 208, Ex. 273: the counter-line changes from half notes to quarters to push into the next phrase): 74 Vc/Vla A flat-G-F-A flat under the tune's held F (the 7th resolves to G at 75).
% c094 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 51: in soft passages one string section = one wind; the counter-melody in unison (Vla + Vc) outweighs the tune spread in octaves): 67 Vla and Vc mp, crescendo 76 to mf at 77 with the tune's crescendo.
% c097 (texture; Adler ch. 11, PDF 44, 48-49: 'the wonderful independence' of a counter-line against the theme): 70-73 the violas' and cellos' counter-melody no longer lands on the tune's pitch (octaves C-C at 70, A flat-A flat at 71, G-G at 73 on the downbeat); it now moves E flat-F-G | E flat-C-E flat | ... | B flat-C-B flat, in sixths and tenths under the tune; 75-82 unchanged. Same change at 190-193 (recS).
% c281 (melody; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 60-61 e): violins and cellos in octaves 'in very expressive passages where the 'cellos have to play on the A or D strings ... a more resonant tone'
%   (Ex. 29 Antar, Ex. 30 Scheherazade III, PDF 206-208); PDF 62 a): Vln I / Vln II / Vc in double octaves 'for full cantabile melodies extremely tense in character, and in forte passages';
%   PDF 54: crossing the cellos above the violas gives the melody relief): 77-81 the cellos leave the counter-melody (the violas keep it) and take F2's climb, peak and suspension an octave
%   below Vln II, E flat4-F4 | B flat4 (A string) ... G3-F3-D3, mf < f > as the violins; 82 back on the counter, mp < with the violas. F2's notes are unchanged.
%   78 beats 3-4 the violas step E flat4-G3 (was E flat4-D4), so the tune's A flat4-G4 in the cellos does not make fifths with them (D4 stays in Hn 1; B flat3 would make octaves with Cl 1 into 79).
% c382 (harmony; A&S Unit 13, PDF 200-201, 204-205, Ex. 13-15, 13-20i: V7-IV6 deceptive cadence, IV6 "temporarily turning the 7th of V7 into a consonance";
%   Unit 11, PDF 170, Ex. 11-12d: IV6-V(7), the 6th of IV6 prepared from the 7th; PDF 201: VI/IV6 for I forestalls repeated tonics; Unit 12, PDF 191: phrase overlap):
%   82 F2's last cadence in E flat no longer resolves V7-I a bar before the closing theme's tonic; 81 V7 -> 82.1-3 IV6 (bass C: Cb, Vc, Bsn 2, harp; Bsn 1 holds
%   the seventh A flat3 as the consonant root; E flat doubled, the 3rd above the bass, in Hn 1-2 and the tune) -> 82.4 V (Vla D4 leading tone, Bsn 1 F4) -> 83 I,
%   so the closing group's downbeat is the cadence goal (elision). Tune, run, dynamics and 83 unchanged. Moves c362's incomplete tonic with tripled root from 82 to 83.
% c402 (harmony; A&S Unit 23, PDF 387-388 sec. 2 and Ex. 23-2b ("minIV6": flat 6 in the bass falling to 5), PDF 388 ("once introduced, flat 6 tends to move to 5;
%   it will not normally be replaced by natural 6 before 5 is reached", Ex. 23-2h), PDF 387 (the active 6-5 of minor brought into major), PDF 395 point 2a):
%   82 the IV6 before V is now minor iv6 over C flat (Vc, Cb, Bn 2, harp; violas double the cellos' C flat-B flat an octave up, as they double the cellos at 67-76), so the C flat of 81
%   is not contradicted by a C natural bass one beat later; Hn 1 E flat4-D4 gives V its third on 82.4 (the violas' D4 moved to it). Harp pedal C flat at 81 (no C in 81; next harp entry 123 has its own diagram).
%   F2's tune, rhythm, dynamics and the closing group at 83 unchanged; the A flat-major statement's iv with F flat at 197.4-198 is the recapitulation's counterpart.
sTwoVc = { g2(\mp^\markup \italic "arco, espress." aes4 bes4) | aes2( g2) | aes4( bes4 c'2) | es'4( f'4 e'2) | es'2( c'4 es'4) | d'2( aes2) | g2( c'2) | bes4(\< aes4 f4\> g4~ | g2\! aes4 bes4) | aes2(\< g2) | \clef tenor es'2.\mf\< f'4\upbow | bes'4.(\f\downbow aes'8 g'4 f'4) | es'2.( d'4) | c'4( bes4 aes4 g4~) | g4( f4\> d2) | \clef bass ces4--\mp\< ces4-- bes,4-- bes,4-- | }
% c239 (v229 comparison; finale.md item 3 and c203: the violas' first E natural is E4 at 97, then 219-220 and 223): 70 beats 3-4 the violas take G4 (the v229 note), the cellos keep E4,
% the third of c232's V4/3 of ii, so the chord stays complete and the violas' thread pitch E4 is not heard early.
% c254: 81 the violas diminish with the violins (from 81.2), 82 mp < into the mf of 83 (clos.ily).
% c412 (A&S Unit 25, PDF 419, 439 point 1: any triad may be preceded by its applied V; lost PDF page 432 and Ex. 25-21b: I -> I flat-7 = V7 of IV -> IV; PDF 423, 439 point 3: do not double the temporary leading tone or the 7th):
%   77.1-2 F2's climb to its B flat 6 climax: the I6 over the bass G becomes V6/5 of IV (G-B flat-D flat-E flat), so the bass G->A flat rises as a leading tone into IV at 77.3.
%   Violas D flat 4 (was B flat 3), the 7th, falls to C4 in their own line; Hn 2 B flat 3-A flat 3 (was G3-F3, a doubled leading tone) with Bn 1; harp RH drops its G4 on 77.1-2. Tune, bass and dynamics unchanged.
% c589 (A&S Unit 22, PDF 375 and Ex. 22-3c: a diminished 5th between upper voices that moves in similar motion to a perfect 5th makes obtrusive hidden 5ths, avoid): 72 the violas hold D4 to beat 3 and pass to the 9th C4 on beat 4, so D4/A flat4 (Vln II) no longer falls to C4/G4 in close position with the tune's passing G; the 9th and 7th still resolve in parallel 3rds into 73 (Ex. 27-2).
sTwoVla = { g2(\mp^\markup \italic "arco, unis., espress." aes4 bes4) | aes2( g2) | aes4( bes4 c'2) | es'4( f'4 g'2) | es'2( c'4 es'4) | d'2.( c'4) | bes2( c'2) | bes4(\< aes4 f4\> g4~ | g2\! aes4 bes4) | aes2(\< g2) | des'2(\mf c'4 f'4) | es'1 | c'2( aes4 c'4) | es'2( d'2) | aes4( ces'4\> bes4 f4) | ces'2\mp\< bes2 | }
% c098 (expression; balance after c094): 67 the basses mp with the violas and cellos (were mf, a level above the counter-melody c094 lowered to mp), crescendo 76 to mf at 77 with them.
% c403 (A&S PDF 255-257 sec. 8, PDF 309-310 Ex. 19-4 bar 2, 19-9): 75 passing V6/4 on F between I6 and I; bass B flat-A flat-G-F-E flat (74-75) then D-E flat an octave lower (Cb, harp; Bn 2 G3-F3-E flat3); Hn 2 G3-B flat3 tied into 76 (its G3-F3 made octaves with the new bass G-F)
sTwoCb = { es,1\mp^\markup \italic "arco" | d,2 es,2 | c,2. bes,,4 | aes,,2 g,,2 | f,,1 | bes,,1 | es,2 aes,,4 a,,4 | bes,,2 aes,,2 | g,,2 f,,4 es,,4 | d,,2\< es,,2 | g,,2\mf aes,,2 | c,1 | aes,,1 | a,,2 bes,,2 | bes,,1 | ces,4--\< ces,4-- bes,,4-- bes,,4-- | }  % c115: stray \ottava #0 removed (no octave sign is open on the Cb staff)
% c216: see sTwoBsnOne.
% c485 (Gould, Behind Bars PDF 52, 66-67: the upper part takes up-stems even when the parts cross; overlapping semibreves are divided into stemmed notes to show part progression; a semibreve must not appear joined to a stem): at 68 Bn 1's B flat2 under Bn 2's D3-E flat3 is written as tied up-stemmed halves in the score (apart), so it no longer merges into Bn 2's D3 head; at 69 Bn 2's dotted-half C3 stands to the right of Bn 1's semibreve C3, clear of it, with its dot beside it. Score only; part and MIDI unchanged.
sTwoBsnTwo = { es1\p | \tag #'score { \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #0 } d2 es2 \tag #'score { \revert NoteColumn.force-hshift } | \tag #'score { \once \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #1.9 } c2. bes,4 | aes,2 g,2 | f,1 | bes,1 | es2 aes,4 a,4 | bes,2. r4 | g2 f4 es4 | d2 es2 | g,2\< aes,2 | c1\mp | aes,1 | a,2 bes,2\> | bes,1\p | ces2\< bes,2 \breathe | }
% c114 (orchestration; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 129-130: the scale anacrusis leads cleanly to the stressed note; PDF 51: plucked strings sound apart from bowed strings): 82 the harp rests on beats 3-4; its E flat arpeggio struck G4 and E flat5 against the run's A flat4 and D5 (Vln II, Ob 1) and D6 (Vln I, Fl 1).
% c114 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 130: in a crescendo the brass join last and swell most easily): 82 Hn 1-2 crescendo with the strings and wood-wind (they held p under the mf < run) and release at 83.
% c116 (playability; Adler ch. 4, PDF 117 sons étouffés: a harp string rings until it is damped): 82 beat 3 étouffez, so the RH E flat arpeggio (G4, E flat5) stops under the run's A flat4 and D5 as c114 intended; the LH E flat2 rings on into 83.
% c116: "(low D string: D♮)" removed at 67: no part of the harp now uses the two unpedalled strings (C1, D1; Adler PDF 111 footnote), since 242 takes D2.
% c296 (playability; Adler ch. 4, adler1 PDF 111: one pedal sets every string of a letter name, so the harp cannot give A natural while its A strings are A flat; an undamped harp string rings on, PDF 116-117):
%   73.3-4 RH E flat4-C5-E flat5-F5 (was E flat4-A flat4-C5-F5): the A flat4 plucked on 73.3 rang into 73.4, where the basses and Bn 2 take A natural (c273, V6/5 of V);
%   E flat, C and F belong to both IV and V6/5 of V. The A flat stays in the bass on 73.3 (Cb, Bn 2, harp LH A flat1, which the written rest on 73.4 damps).
% c352 (A&S Unit 27, PDF 463, 469-470, Ex. 27-10, 27-11): 81 beat 2, the harp no longer strikes B flat4 against the violas' minor-ninth neighbour C flat4 (its tone of resolution a major seventh above it); first group F4-A flat4-D5-F5.
% c442 (harmony; A&S Unit 27, PDF 469-470 sec. 7 and Ex. 27-10, Unit 21 sec. 12: a 9th is unclear when its tone of resolution sounds in another upper voice at the same time; as c352 at 81): 72 beats 3-4, the harp's second group is F4-A flat4-D5-F5 (was F4-A flat4-B flat4-D5), so no B flat4 sounds a seventh above the violas' 9th C4 before it falls to B flat3 at 73. No new pedal change.
% c511 (A&S Unit 21 sec. 12, PDF 354, Ex. 21-12: avoid sounding the tone of resolution against a suspension): 81.1 the harp's first eighth is G4 (was F4), doubling the tune's suspended G instead of sounding its resolution F a whole tone below Vln II's G4 and in the octave of the cellos' and Hn 1's G3 (the reason c398 moved Hn 2 off F3; c352's group F4-A flat4-D5-F5 kept its B flat out, and still does); the harp's low voice now runs F-F | G-F | E flat, the 6-5 itself.
sTwoHarpR = { \stemUp es'8 g'8 bes'8 es''8 es'8 g'8 bes'8 es''8 | f'8 aes'8 bes'8 d''8 es'8 g'8 bes'8 es''8 | es'8 aes'8 c''8 es''8 es'8 aes'8 c''8 es''8 | es'8 aes'8 c''8 es''8 c'8 g'8 bes'8 c''8 | es'8 f'8 aes'8 c''8 es'8 f'8 aes'8 c''8 | f'8 bes'8 d''8 f''8 f'8 aes'8 d''8 f''8 | es'8 g'8 bes'8 es''8 es'8 c''8 es''8 f''8 | f'8 bes'8 d''8 f''8 f'8 aes'8 bes'8 d''8 | es'8 g'8 bes'8 es''8 f'8 d'8 g'8 bes'8 | f'8 aes'8 bes'8 d''8 es'8 g'8 bes'8 es''8 | bes8 es'8 bes'8 es''8 es'8 f'8 aes'8 c''8 | es'8 g'8 c''8 es''8 es'8 c''8 es''8 aes''8 | es'8 aes'8 c''8 es''8 es'8 aes'8 c''8 es''8 | f'8 c''8 es''8 f''8 f'8 bes'8 d''8 f''8 | g'8 aes'8 d''8 f''8 f'8 aes'8 bes'8 d''8 | es'8 aes'8 ces''8 es''8 r2^\markup \italic "étouffez" \stemNeutral | }
% c039 (Gould PDF 45: across extensive rests the notes take separate octave signs): the 8 bassa line closes at 82; it ran on over rests to 186.
% c075 (MOLA PDF 5): pedal diagram score-only (tag 'score)
% c296 (Adler PDF 117, damping): 82 LH E flat2 is a half note (was a whole note), so the left hand ends where the RH's "étouffez" damps the harp on 82.3; the basses keep the E flat.
% c335 (Gould PDF 44: the octave line ends with a corner straight after the last notehead, not at the end of the duration or across a following rest; regression from c296's es,2 r2): ottava closed by a spacer an eighth after the E flat
sTwoHarpL = { \ottava #-1 \set Staff.ottavation = #"8" es,1\mf-\tag #'score ^\markup \small \harp-pedal #"--^|^--^" | d,2 es,2 | c,2. bes,,4 | aes,,2 g,,2 | f,,1 | bes,,1 | es,2 aes,,4 r4 | bes,,2 aes,,2 | g,,2 f,,4 es,,4 | d,,2 es,,2 | g,,2 aes,,2 | c,1 | aes,,1 | r2 bes,,2 | bes,,1-\tag #'score _\markup \small "C♭" | << ces,2 { s8 \ottava #0 } >> r2 | }
% c113 (form; Dvorak 9/iv 89-92: the second theme's last phrase crescendos f< into the ff codetta at 92 with no held tonic bar; this score's recap seam 196-198 crescendos into 199 (c068)): 82 the violins, Fl 1 and Ob 1 no longer hold the theme's last E flat for a whole bar under the animando and a diminuendo; they take it as a half note mf and run up G-A flat-B flat-D (crescendo) into the closing group's E flat 6/5 at 83 f; violas, cellos, basses and bassoons crescendo through 82. The animando of 81-82 now has eighth-note motion to carry it.
% c047 (texture; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108: clarinets should rarely be set inside the horns; two horns and two bassoons give the soft four-part chord): Cl 1-2 rest 75-82, where Hn 1-2 enter; the pad passes from clarinets to horns
% c149 (Rimsky PDF 141, Ex. 280-281: a climax is supported harmonically in the register in which the melody is placed; PDF 159: two-part writing in 3rds and 6ths): 77-79 Cl 1-2 fill the gap of an octave and a fifth between the pad (Eb4) and the F2 peak (Bb5/Bb6) with the harmony in 3rds, p< f > like Fl 1/Ob 1; they double the bassoon/horn pad an octave higher and rest again before their closing-theme entry at 83.
% c291 (melody; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 69: a melody in three octaves keeps the natural order Fl / Ob / Cl; PDF 76-77: 'Cellos + Cl.' is a natural unison, one wind to one string group so the strings still lead;
% PDF 67: placing parts across each other's register 'creates an unnatural resonance'; PDF 78, Servilia [93]: each octave of a string melody with its own wind): since c281 the cellos play F2 an octave below Vln II at 77-81,
% so the gap that c149's clarinet thirds filled (E flat4 to B flat5) now holds the tune's lowest octave, and the thirds B flat4/G4 - A flat4/F4 - G4/E flat4 - B flat4/F4 - C5/A flat4 crossed it (Cl 2 on the cellos' F4 at 77.4 and 78.4,
% Cl 1 B flat4 over the cellos' G4-F4 at 78.3). Cl 1 now doubles the cellos at pitch for 77-80, as Ob 1 doubles Vln II and Fl 1 doubles Vln I, with Ob 1's slurs, breath and dynamics; Cl 2 rests 77-79.
% F2's peak is in three octaves, each a string section plus one wind (Fl + Vln I / Ob + Vln II / Cl + Vc). F2's notes unchanged.
sTwoClOne = { R1*10 | es'2.(\p\< f'4 | bes'4.\mf aes'8 g'4 f'4) \breathe | es'2.( d'4 | c'4 bes4 aes4 g4)\> | r1\! | R1 | }
sTwoClTwo = { g4 r4 r2 | R1*9 | R1*6 | }  % c291: Cl 2 rests 77-82 (its thirds crossed the cellos' F2 octave; Cl 1 doubles the cellos)
% c147 (texture; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. III, PDF 108: soft four-part harmony in two horns and two bassoons, crossed, 'the concords being given to the horns, the discords to the bassoons'; bassoons may stand inside the horns, not the reverse; Ex. 152, PDF 324, Antar: the horn basis holds while the wood-wind moves): 75-82 Bn 1 is a part of its own instead of Hn 1 an octave lower (it doubled Hn 1 at the octave in all eight bars, so the 'four-part' pad had three parts): Bn 1 B flat3 inside the horns at 75, the bass at 76, B flat3-A flat3 inside the horns at 77, E flat3-F3 at 78 (F, the fourth over the C bass, in the bassoon), E flat3 at 79, E flat3-B flat3 at 80, the seventh A flat3 at 81 inside the horns (D4/F3), resolving to G3 at 82.
% c216 (Adler PDF 175, 226): 82 Bn 1-2 breathe before the f closing theme at 83 (75-86 is otherwise 12 bars without a rest), as Hn 1-2 do since c145.
% c272 (A&S Unit 5, PDF 80 and 89 point 6: inner voices move mostly by step and repeated tones; PDF 81 Ex. 5-11b; PDF 76: doublings for smoother voice leading): 76 Bn 1 holds B flat3 from 75 into 77 (root of V6/5, then fifth of I) instead of joining Bn 2 on the bass D3-E flat3, so its pad line is B flat3 (75-77.2) - A flat3 (77.3) with no minor-sixth drop and fifth climb; Bn 2, the basses and the harp keep the bass.
% c353 (A&S Unit 15, PDF 243 sec. 12 and PDF 245 review point 10: a diminished triad on the leading tone is seldom satisfactory in 5/3 position; a leading tone in the bass should usually support V6/5, which contains all three of its tones; PDF 243: in the diminished chord double the third, not the leading tone):
%   68.1 had D-F-A flat over the bass D with the leading tone in Cb, Bn 1 and Bn 2 and the root B flat only as the harp's third arpeggio eighth; Bn 1 now holds B flat2 through 68 (fifth of I at 68.3), so F2's second bar is V6/5 as at 52, 60, 76 and 188.
sTwoBsnOne = { es1\p | \partCombineApart \tag #'score { \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #1.3 bes,2~ bes,2 \revert NoteColumn.force-hshift } \tag #'part { bes,1 } | \tag #'score { \once \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #0 } c1 \partCombineAutomatic | c2. r4 | c1 | d1 | es2 c4 es4 | d1 | bes2 f4 bes4~ | bes1~ | bes2\< aes2 | es2\mp f4 r4 | es1 | f2 bes2\> | aes1\p~ | aes2\< g4 f4 \breathe | }
% c147 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108, as above; A&S PDF 76, 83: the leading tone is not doubled): the horns keep concords (thirds, fourths, fifths, sixths) and no seventh: 76 Hn 1 F4-E flat4 (was D4-E flat4, the bass's leading tone in octaves with the bass E flat-D) over Hn 2 B flat3-G3 (was the seventh A flat3, a tritone under Hn 1's D4; the seventh is in the violas and cellos); 77 Hn 2 G3-F3 (was G3-A flat3 in octaves with the bass G-A flat); 79 Hn 1 E flat4 over Hn 2 C4 (was a unison C4); 80 Hn 2 A flat3-F3 (was A flat3-B flat3 in octaves with the bass); 81 Hn 2 F3 (was A flat3: the tritone D4/A flat3 in the horns); 82 Hn 1 B flat3, Hn 2 E flat3 (Hn 1 D4-E flat4 moved in octaves with the violins' and flutes' D-E flat).
% c148 (expression; A&S Unit 3, PDF 53: 75-78 is one four-bar group whose goal is the dotted peak at 78; recS 197-198, where the pad crescendos with the tune): 77 Hn 1-2 and Bn 1-2 crescendo with the violins to mp at 78 (they held p under the f peak), back to p at 81 with the violins' diminuendo; 82's crescendo into 83 unchanged.
% c239 (Rimsky ch. IV, PDF 136: after a long rest horns re-enter pp or ff, a p entry is weaker; PDF 127: an added group enters pp and grows): 75 Hn 1-2 enter pp (24 bars' rest), crescendo to mp at 78 unchanged.
% c287 (texture; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 79: "the combination of horns and 'cellos ... produces a beautifully blended, soft quality of tone";
%   PDF 76: one wind with a string group, the strings predominate; PDF 61-62 Ex. 90 Sheherazade: cellos + horns on a line): 67-74 Hn 1 doubles the
%   cellos' counter-melody at pitch, pp dolce (the violas' G4 at 70.3, not the cellos' E4, so the violas' E4 thread is not underlined); breath after 74,
%   then the pad from 75 as before (Hn 2 still enters pp at 75). The recapitulation keeps Bn 1 on the counter (187-194), so the two statements differ in colour.
% c297 (Adler ch. 11, adler2 PDF 50 and 52-54, Ex. XI-29 (Druckman): open and closed brass must be marked exactly in every part, 'some instruments are open and some closed'): the first horn note after a stopped passage carries 'o' (open), as retr 139 has: Hn 1 at 67 (stopped 49-50), Hn 1 at 187 and Hn 2 at 195 (stopped 168-169).
% c341 (Adler ch. 11, adler2 PDF 27, Ex. XI-10, Strauss Don Quixote: 'Usually the horn and cello double on long sustained melodies'; the horns add articulation to the cello line):
%   77-80 Hn 1 leaves its pad and doubles the cellos (and Cl 1) at pitch on F2's climax phrase E flat4-F4 | B flat4. A flat G F | E flat4. D4 | C B flat A flat G, breathing with Cl 1 after 78;
%   dynamics as before (cresc. from 77, mp at 78, dim. from 80.3, p at 81), equal with Hn 2 on the shared staff. Hn 2's pad, the tune and every other part unchanged.
% c425 (Gould, Behind Bars PDF 21-23: stay in one clef while at most three ledger lines are needed, change clef between phrases, never during a tie): score-only bass clef for Hn 1-2 at 79-82, as c095 at 25-26 and 165-166.
%   At concert pitch Hn 2's B flat2 at 81-82 (c398, c403) stood on five ledger lines in treble; in bass clef 79-82 needs at most two. Clef at 79 after Hn 1's breath (78), treble again at 83 after the breath at 82.
%   The F parts keep the treble clef (B flat2 reads F3 there, three ledger lines). MIDI unchanged.
% c595 (Adler part 2 PDF 294 and 301 Ex. XVIII-10a: one staff, one set of dynamics unless the players need different ones): 81 Hn 1 reaches p on 81.1 with Hn 2 (and Bn 1-2), so the shared staff prints one hairpin and one p instead of p at 81.1 and a second p at 81.3.
sTwoHnOne = { g2(\pp-\open-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." ^\markup \italic "dolce" aes4 bes4 | aes2 g2) | aes4( bes4 c'2 | es'4 f'4 g'2) | es'2( c'4 es'4 | d'2 aes2) | g2( c'2 | bes4 aes4 f4 g4) \breathe | es'2\pp d'4 es'4 | f'2 es'2 | es'2.(\< f'4 | bes'4.\mp aes'8 g'4 f'4) \breathe | \tag #'score \clef bass es'2.( d'4 | c'4 bes4 aes4\> g4~ | g4\p f4) d'2 | es'2.\< d'4 \breathe | \tag #'score \clef treble }
sTwoHnTwo = { R1*6 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #DOWN { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | g2\pp bes2~ | bes2 g2 | bes2\< aes2 | g2\mp aes2 | \tag #'score \clef bass c'1 | f2~ f2\> | bes,1\p | es2\< bes,2 \breathe | \tag #'score \clef treble }
% c279 (Adler ch. 16, adler2 PDF 217 and 224, Ravel Petit Poucet: the melody passes to a new instrument without overlap, the background dovetails; the clarinet holds a note into the
%   next bar "comparable to the damper pedal"): 66-67 Cl 2 ties its G natural3 (the major third reached at 66.3) into 67.1 under the violas' and cellos' entry on the same G3,
%   so the background crosses the seam while the tune changes from trumpet to violins with a fresh attack.

% c398 (expression; A&S Unit 21, PDF 354 sec. 12 and Ex. 21-12a-c: the tone of resolution sounding in another voice before the suspension resolves 'produces an unclear sound and weakens the effect of the resolution', tolerable only with marked contrast of register, timbre or dynamics or as a 9-8 over the bass; PDF 347 sec. 1, Ex. 21-2: a double suspension moves as one figure; PDF 348 sec. 4: the suspension is stressed and its resolution softer):
%   81 Hn 2 B flat2 (the V7's root) instead of holding F3: since c341 (Hn 1 on the cellos' line) and c389 (Hn 1's tied G3) the 7/6 suspension G3 in Hn 1 and the cellos sounded a second above Hn 2's F3 on the same staff, then fell into unison with it; c241's reason (the F an octave or more lower, in another timbre) held only for the violins' and flute's G. Hn 2 F3 -> B flat2 -> E flat3 (82), oblique to the bass B flat1.
%   81 Ob 1's diminuendo starts on its resolution D5 (81.2), as c248 set it for the tune's G-F a third above (Vln I, Vln II, Fl 1), so the double suspension E flat5/G5 is shaped as one figure (it began on the tied E flat at 81.1).
% c389 (A&S Unit 21, PDF 346-348, Ex. 21-2 Bach Sarabande: double suspension in parallel 3rds, 'suspensions bring into prominence the voice in which they occur'): 80.4-81 Ob 1 takes E flat5 on 80.4, tied into 81 and resolving to D5 on 81.2 with the tune's suspended G5-F5 (c241), a 6-5 over 4-3 double suspension over V7; Hn 1 ties its G3 into 81 with the tune (G3-F3) instead of sounding D4, the resolution tone, against the suspended E flat; D4 at 81.3.
